<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Husam al-Din Timurtash</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1122">1122</a>–<a href="/years/1154">1154</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Husam al-Din Timurtash was the Artuqid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a> from 1122 to 1154, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilghazi">Ilghazi</a>. He briefly held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> after his father's death, released the captive King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-of-jerusalem">Baldwin II of Jerusalem</a> for a ransom in 1124, and kept Mardin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayyafariqin">Mayyafariqin</a> by balancing between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty">Zengids</a> and other neighbouring powers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/husam-al-din-timurtash.jpg" alt="Husam al-Din Timurtash" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artuqids_(Mardin)._Husam_al-Din_Timurtash._AH_516-547_AD_1122-1152._Diademed,_Seleukid-style_bust_right._Name_and_titles_of_Timurtash_in_four_lines_and_in_margins.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born around 1105, Timurtash was still very young when in 1111/12 he was sent as an envoy to the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-tapar">Muhammad I Tapar</a> to plead for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubays-ibn-sadaqa">Dubays ibn Sadaqa</a>. In 1118 he went with his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilghazi">Ilghazi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and governed the city for a time while Ilghazi sought reinforcements at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a>. When Ilghazi died in 1122, Timurtash took Mardin and his brother Shams al-Dawla Suleiman took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayyafariqin">Mayyafariqin</a>. The chief sources for his life are the chronicles of Ibn al-Azraq al-Fariqi and Ibn al-Athir, with Michael the Syrian, Ibn al-Furat, Al-Azimi and Ibn al-Adim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belek-ghazi">Belek Ghazi</a> was killed at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manbij">Manbij</a> on 6 May 1124, and Timurtash carried his body back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and took over the city. Through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>, the emir of Shaizar, he agreed terms for the release of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-of-jerusalem">Baldwin II of Jerusalem</a>: 80,000 dinars and the cession of Atarib, Zardana, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azaz">Azaz</a> and other fortresses. Baldwin was freed on 29 August 1124, leaving hostages who included his daughter Ioveta and Joscelin II. Defeated outside Aleppo by Sultan-Shah and Dubays, allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>, Timurtash withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a>, and in 1125 Aleppo passed to Aqsunqur al-Bursuqi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>.</p><p>The rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Imad al-Din Zengi</a> dominated the rest of his reign. Zengi took Nusaybin from him by replacing a message carried by pigeon, defeated an Artuqid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> alliance at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saruj">Saruj</a> in 1130, and later took Dara and a daughter of Timurtash in marriage when Timurtash refused to hand over a fugitive governor. He also forced Timurtash to return peasants who had left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a>, where, Timurtash said, he took only a tenth of the harvest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When Zengi was assassinated in 1146, Timurtash at once seized Hani, Siwan and Siirt. In 1148/49 he suffered a heavy defeat by Saif al-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, who died in 1149 before achieving anything decisive. Kara Arslan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a> then recognised Timurtash's overlordship, and around 1150 Timurtash took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsat">Samsat</a>. In 1152 the caliph Al-Muqtafi sent him robes and diplomas recognising his possessions. He died in 1154 and was succeeded by his son Najm al-Din Alpi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Husam al-Din Timurtash” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Timurtash</b> and later rulers held Mardin and Mayyafariqin, survived by accepting Zengid, Ayyubid and then Ilkhanid overlordship, and kept their dynasty intact through repeated invasions.</blockquote><a href="/state/artuqids-of-mardin">Artuqids of Mardin</a></li></ul></section>
